## Claim
Text: "Republican Matt Gaetz was part of a company that had to pay 75 million in hospice fraud. They stole from dying people."
Claim date: October 31, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Is Matt Gaetz a member of the Republican Party?
Answer: Yes, Matt Gaetz is a member of the Republican Party. The source explicitly identifies him as "Rep. Matt Gaetz, a Republican from the Florida Panhandle," and further notes his role in the "GOP primary" and his affiliation with the Republican establishment in Florida's 1st Congressional District.

Source URL: https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2019/07/how-matt-gaetz-used-daddys-money-to-become-trumps-favorite-congressman/

### Was Matt Gaetz himself personally affiliated with a company that was involved in hospice fraud?
Answer: Based on the search result, Matt Gaetz himself does not appear to have been personally affiliated with the hospice company Vitas. The article indicates that it was his father, Don Gaetz, and his partners who founded and sold the hospice company Vitas in 2004 for $400 million. The article specifically notes that "Don Gaetz wasn't named in the case and has denied any wrongdoing" regarding the $75 million Medicare fraud settlement. While the Gaetz family made their money from Vitas, the search result does not provide evidence that Matt Gaetz himself was personally part of or affiliated with the company involved in the hospice fraud.

Source URL: https://www.pnj.com/story/opinion/2019/07/20/marlette-mother-jones-kicks-open-gaetz/1761716001/

### What is the name of the company that allegedly paid $75 million to settle hospice fraud allegations?
Answer: The company named in the hospice fraud case connected to the Gaetz family is **Vitas Hospice** (also referred to as Vitas Healthcare), a subsidiary of Chemed Corp. According to the article, the Department of Justice "is suing the hospice company founded by Florida's Senate president [Don Gaetz, Matt Gaetz's father], accusing it of submitting tens of millions of dollars in fraudulent Medicare claims for more than a decade, including while Don Gaetz was vice chairman of the board." However, this source describes a 2013 lawsuit rather than a confirmed $75 million settlement.

Source URL: https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud

### Did the company pay exactly $75 million to settle the hospice fraud allegations, or was the amount different?
Answer: According to the search result, the company did pay exactly $75 million. The PolitiFact article states: "In 2017, the government settled the case for $75 million." It also references a U.S. Department of Justice press release titled "Chemed Corp. and Vitas Hospice Services Agree to Pay $75 Million to Resolve False Claims Act Allegations Relating to Billing for Ineligible Patients and Inflated Levels of Care, Oct. 30, 2017." So while the $75 million figure in the claim is accurate, the claim is misleading because it was Don Gaetz's (Matt Gaetz's father's) former company, Vitas Healthcare Corporation, that paid the settlement — not Matt Gaetz's own company.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/nov/01/facebook-posts/post-says-rep-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-wrong/

### What was Matt Gaetz's specific role, position, or connection to the company that committed hospice fraud?
Answer: According to the source, Matt Gaetz hadno direct connection to the company that committed hospice fraud. The company in question was Vitas Healthcare Corporation (later Vitas Hospice Services LLC), which was "a company that was once owned by Matt Gaetz's father Don Gaetz." The source explicitly states: "It didn't involve Matt Gaetz, and the elder Gaetz was not named as a defendant in the lawsuit." Don Gaetz sold Vitas in 2004 to Chemed, and the $75 million settlement in 2017 was for Medicare fraud alleged to have occurred between 2002 and 2013, but neither Matt Gaetz nor his father Don Gaetz were defendants in the case.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/nov/01/facebook-posts/post-says-rep-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-wrong/

### Was the company that committed hospice fraud connected to Matt Gaetz's father, Don Gaetz, rather than to Matt Gaetz directly?
Answer: Yes, the search result confirms that the company involved in the hospice fraud was Vitas Hospice, which was founded by and connected to **Don Gaetz** (Matt Gaetz's father), not Matt Gaetz directly. The article states that Don Gaetz founded Vitas in the early 1980s, served as vice chairman of the board during part of the time federal investigators alleged the fraud took place, and was identified as "Florida's Senate president." The article makes no mention of Matt Gaetz's involvement with the company, indicating the original claim misattributed the connection.

Source URL: https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud

### When did the hospice fraud allegedly occur, and when was the $75 million settlement reached?
Answer: According to the source, the hospice fraud allegedly occurred between 2002 and 2013, when the U.S. Justice Department accused Vitas Healthcare Corporation of engaging in Medicare fraud through false billings for hospice services. The $75 million settlement was reached in 2017, as stated: "In 2017, the government settled the case for $75 million."

Source URL: https://www.tampabay.com/florida-politics/buzz/2019/11/04/politifact-facebook-post-says-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-its-wrong/

### What were the specific allegations of fraud made against the company, and what government agency brought the case?
Answer: According tothe search result, the Department of Justice (DOJ) brought the case against Vitas Hospice and Vitas Healthcare, alleging that the company "submitted claims for emergency services for patients that weren't needed, weren't provided, or were provided to patients who weren't eligible under Medicare requirements." The lawsuit further claimed that "the companies set goals for the number of crisis-care days to be billed and pressured their employees to submit more claims so it would get more revenue," with these fraudulent Medicare claims allegedly occurring "for more than a decade."

Source URL: https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud

### Did the hospice fraud involve taking money intended for patients who were receiving end-of-life or hospice care?
Answer: Yes, the hospice fraud involved money intended for end-of-life or hospice care. According to the source, the Justice Department alleged that "between 2002 and 2013, the company had defrauded Medicare by filing false claims for services never provided or for patients who weren't terminally ill," and the company "settled the case in 2017 for more than $75 million." Medicare hospice payments are specifically funds intended for terminally ill patients receiving end-of-life care, so the fraud involved taking money meant for this purpose.

Source URL: https://www.pnj.com/story/opinion/2019/07/20/marlette-mother-jones-kicks-open-gaetz/1761716001/

### Was Matt Gaetz himself accused of criminal wrongdoing, civil liability, or regulatory violation in connection with the hospice fraud case?
Answer: No. According to the search result, Matt Gaetz was not named as a defendant in the hospice fraud case and was not personally accused of any wrongdoing. The lawsuit was filed by the U.S. Justice Department against Chemed Corporation and its hospice subsidiaries, including Vitas Healthcare Corporation—a company once owned by Matt Gaetz's father, Don Gaetz, who had sold it in 2004. As PolitiFact notes, "The case did not name either Matt or Don Gaetz as a defendant," and Don Gaetz "denied wrongdoing." The fraud pertained to Medicare billings between 2002 and 2013 and resulted in a $75 million settlement in 2017, with no individual charges against Matt Gaetz.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/nov/01/facebook-posts/post-says-rep-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-wrong/

## Final Judgement
## Summary

The evidence gathered through multiple reliable sources (PolitiFact, Mother Jones, Deseret News, Tampa Bay Times, PNJ) consistently shows that the $75 million hospice fraud settlement was paid by **Vitas Healthcare Corporation** (a subsidiary of Chemed Corp), which was founded and once owned by Matt Gaetz's **father, Don Gaetz**. Don Gaetz sold the company in 2004, and the alleged fraud occurred between 2002 and 2013. Critically, multiple sources explicitly state that **Matt Gaetz was not named as a defendant, was not personally affiliated with the company, and had no direct connection** to the hospice fraud case. While Matt Gaetz is indeed a Republican, and the $75 million figure is accurate, the claim's core assertion—that Matt Gaetz was "part of" the company—is explicitly refuted by the evidence.

## Missing Information

No further information is needed. The evidence is clear and consistent across multiple independent sources.

## Conclusion

The claim that "Matt Gaetz was part of a company that had to pay 75 million in hospice fraud" is explicitly refuted by the evidence. The company in question was his father Don Gaetz's former company, which Don Gaetz had sold years before the settlement. Matt Gaetz was never a part of, named in, or personally connected to the hospice fraud case. The claim misattributes the company to Matt Gaetz when it was actually his father's, making the central assertion factually false.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because the $75 million hospice fraud settlement was paid by **Vitas Healthcare Corporation** (a Chemed Corp subsidiary), which was founded and previously owned by Matt Gaetz's **father, Don Gaetz**, not by Matt Gaetz himself. Don Gaetz sold the company in 2004, well before the alleged fraud period (2002–2013) and the 2017 settlement; neither Matt nor Don Gaetz was named as a defendant in the case, and Matt Gaetz had no personal affiliation with the company ([PolitiFact](https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/nov/01/facebook-posts/post-says-rep-matt-gaetz-has-numerous-duis-wrong/), [Mother Jones/PNJ](https://www.pnj.com/story/opinion/2019/07/20/marlette-mother-jones-kicks-open-gaetz/1761716001/), [Deseret News](https://www.deseret.com/2013/5/9/20451919/largest-us-hospice-company-sued-for-medicare-fraud)). While the $75 million figure and the Republican affiliation are accurate, the central assertion that Matt Gaetz was "part of" the company is directly contradicted by the evidence.